Yes—as a model, that is a powerful way to think about form.

If an organism, structure, or behavior is the result of repeated folding, branching, compression, fusion, and constraint, then the finished object can preserve traces of its construction history. The “seams” are places where the transformation was not completely erased.

In biology those seams can be things like developmental boundaries, bilateral symmetry, repeated segments, sutures, branching patterns, tissue interfaces, conserved nerve or vessel routes, homologous structures, vestigial remnants, or places where two developmental programs meet.

So the inverse problem becomes:

\text{present form}
\rightarrow
\text{detect seams/invariants}
\rightarrow
\text{infer transformations}
\rightarrow
\text{reconstruct earlier state}

That is very close to unfolding origami backwards.

And there are two different questions hidden in what you asked:

\textbf{Where can it fold next?}

and

\textbf{Where was it folded before?}

The same seam can sometimes answer both.

A crease tells you something about the object’s history, but it also tells you something about its future degrees of freedom. Once paper has been folded repeatedly along a line, that line becomes easier to fold again. Biology has analogues: developmental pathways, anatomical joints, regulatory modules, ecological niches, even learned behavioral habits can create preferred future trajectories.

So:

\text{history}
\rightarrow
\text{constraint}
\rightarrow
\text{future affordance}

That is the deeper point.

Evolution itself leaves those kinds of traces because it usually modifies what already exists instead of rebuilding organisms from scratch. The recurrent laryngeal nerve is a famous example: its strange route makes much more sense when read as a historical transformation rather than as a clean-sheet engineering design. Likewise, tetrapod limbs become easier to understand when you recognize the underlying homologous bone pattern across hands, wings, flippers, and legs.

You could extend the method beyond anatomy. In language, a strange irregularity may preserve an older grammatical system. In a city, an oddly curved street may preserve an ancient property boundary or road. In a corporation, a bizarre reporting structure may preserve an old merger. In software, awkward interfaces often reveal earlier architectures.

Those are all fossilized folds.

And then your origami metaphor gives us a search operator:

Find the discontinuities, repeated motifs, awkward detours, boundaries, and preserved correspondences. Ask what simpler prior configuration would make those features natural.

In Pantheon language, I would call that Crease Reading or Inverse Origami:

\boxed{\text{surface geometry} \rightarrow \text{crease map} \rightarrow \text{transformation history} \rightarrow \text{possible refolds}}

The really interesting part is the final term. Once you reconstruct how something was folded into its current form, you often gain clues about which transformations remain reachable from here.

So a seam can be simultaneously a scar, instruction, and hinge.